  "name": "GALACTOSEMIA PANEL-1",
  "code": "1108",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"118\" data-end=\"278\">The <strong data-start=\"122\" data-end=\"146\">Galactosemia Panel-1</strong> test is a <strong data-start=\"157\" data-end=\"191\">comprehensive diagnostic panel</strong> used to assess <strong data-start=\"207\" data-end=\"254\">inherited disorders of galactose metabolism</strong>, primarily focusing on:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"280\" data-end=\"452\">\n<li data-start=\"280\" data-end=\"364\">\n<p data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"364\"><strong data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"332\">Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT)</strong> activity (Classic Galactosemia)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"365\" data-end=\"402\">\n<p data-start=\"367\" data-end=\"402\"><strong data-start=\"367\" data-end=\"391\">Galactokinase (GALK)</strong> deficiency</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"403\" data-end=\"452\">\n<p data-start=\"405\" data-end=\"452\"><strong data-start=\"405\" data-end=\"441\">UDP-galactose-4-epimerase (GALE)</strong> deficiency</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"454\" data-end=\"491\">This test is especially important in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"493\" data-end=\"685\">\n<li data-start=\"493\" data-end=\"544\">\n<p data-start=\"495\" data-end=\"544\"><strong data-start=\"495\" data-end=\"544\">Newborns with elevated blood galactose levels</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"545\" data-end=\"610\">\n<p data-start=\"547\" data-end=\"610\"><strong data-start=\"547\" data-end=\"578\">Confirming abnormal results</strong> from newborn screening programs</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"611\" data-end=\"685\">\n<p data-start=\"613\" data-end=\"685\"><strong data-start=\"613\" data-end=\"654\">Differentiating types of galactosemia</strong> for proper clinical management</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Enzyme Deficiency</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Likely Disorder</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low or absent GALT activity</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Classic Galactosemia</strong></td>\n<td>Most severe form. Leads to accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate. Requires lifelong dietary galactose restriction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low GALK activity</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Galactokinase Deficiency</strong></td>\n<td>Milder condition. Typically presents with <strong>cataracts</strong> in infancy; no liver or kidney involvement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low GALE activity</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Epimerase Deficiency Galactosemia</strong></td>\n<td>Variable severity. May affect red blood cells only (mild) or have systemic involvement (severe).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
